      "text": "To solve the problem, we will first calculate the prime factorizations of $5!$ and $\\frac{8!}{3!}$. Then we will identify the common factors using the formula for the greatest common divisor in terms of prime factorization.\n\n1. Calculate $5!$:\n\\[5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120\\]\n\\[ \\text{Prime factorization of } 5! = 2^3 \\cdot 3^1 \\cdot 5^1 \\]\n\n2. Calculate $\\frac{8!}{3!}$:\n\\[8! = 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 8 \\cdot (7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1) = 8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1\\]\n\\[ \\frac{8!}{3!} = \\frac{8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1\\]\n\\[ \\text{Prime factorization of } \\frac{8!}{3!} = 8 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^1 \\cdot 5^1 \\cdot 7^1 \\]\nSince simplifying by 3! can be simplified into 3 prime factors with an exponent 1, this number becomes 8*5 for the primes and without additional factors, it essentially is $2^2 \\cdot 3^1$.\n\n3. Now, in order to find the GCD, look at the common exponents: $2^3$ and $2^2$ means the smallest one will dictate the power, so it is $2^2 = 4$ in total, for the prime 5 both are 1 and common numerator 3 are both 1, so the common factor for digit 1 is 3. Thus, the GCD is $2^2 * 3^1 = 4 * 3 = 12$.\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of $5!$ and $\\frac{8!}{3!}$ is $\\boxed{12}$.",
